# Line endings are NEVER converted in this repository.
#
# WHY: several published facts are sha256 digests of files in this tree, and a
# digest is a statement about bytes. Git's `core.autocrlf` rewrites LF to CRLF
# on checkout — it is the INSTALLER DEFAULT on Windows — which changes those
# bytes and therefore every one of those digests, on a machine where nobody
# involved did anything wrong.
#
# Observed, not reasoned about. Cloning this repo with `core.autocrlf=true`:
#
#   CLA.md                    0dd37a0a…  ->  0b9ece2b…
#   LICENSE                   cfc7749b…  ->  3ddf9be5…
#   plugins/*                 14 of 14 trust-lock entries mismatch
#
# What each of those costs:
#
#   CLA.md      CONTRIBUTING.md asks a contributor to post a sentence naming
#               the digest. It is the only thing tying an acceptance to
#               specific text. A contributor who checks it would find it wrong
#               and be right to conclude the agreement had been changed.
#
#   LICENSE     pyproject.toml states the Apache-2.0 text is verbatim "so
#               anyone can confirm it was not tampered with" — an explicit
#               invitation to verify. Whoever accepts the invitation on
#               Windows is told we tampered with it.
#
#   plugins/    trusted_plugins.lock hashes each bundled plugin's source. A
#               CRLF checkout fails ALL FOURTEEN, so botainer refuses to load
#               its own bundled plugins. This one is a functional break, not a
#               documentation one.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion entirely: working-tree bytes equal
# committed bytes on every platform, so the mismatch is IMPOSSIBLE rather than
# something a contributor has to be warned about. Applied to `*` rather than to
# the three known cases, because the next file whose hash we publish should be
# covered before anyone remembers it needs to be — this project's most-repeated
# defect is the fix that reached one sibling and not the other.
#
# tests/unit/test_line_endings_are_never_converted.py asks git itself to
# resolve the attribute and fails if this file stops taking effect.
* -text
